February (A Calendar of Sonnets); source text begins "Still lie the sheltering snows, undimmed and white;
  And reigns the winter's pregnant silence " and ends "heed,
  To reckon clear the new life's sterner need;
  Fit days, for Feast of Expiation placed!".

To the Thawing Wind; source text begins "Come with rain, O loud Southwester!
Bring the singer, bring the nester;
Give the buried flower " and ends "n the wall;
Run the rattling pages o'er;
Scatter poems on the floor;
Turn the poet out of door.". Scan-backed poem: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Boy%27s_Will/To_the_Thawing_Wind

July (A Calendar of Sonnets); source text begins "Some flowers are withered and some joys have died;
  The garden reeks with an East Indian scent" and ends ".
  Unharmed she lifts her queenly face and head;
  She drinks of living waters and keeps fair.".
